p.src:
Use different dummy labels $k$ and $\ell$ in the two sums. Substitution gives $\begin{aligned}
\mathcal A_{i\to f}
&=g\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}dt
\sum_{\ell,k}
e^{i\omega(\ell)t}e^{-i\omega(k)t}
\langle k_f\rvert a^\dagger(\ell)a(k)\lvert k_i\rangle.\end{aligned}$ Which term in the $k$-sum can survive? An annihilation operator gives zero in every empty mode. The initial state contains a particle only in mode $k_i$, so $k=k_i$. Which term in the $\ell$-sum can survive? Acting to the left, $a^\dagger(\ell)$ must remove the particle represented by the final bra, so $\ell=k_f$. Both sums collapse: $\begin{aligned}
\mathcal A_{i\to f}
&=g\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}dt\,
e^{i(\omega_f-\omega_i)t}
\langle k_f\rvert a^\dagger(k_f)a(k_i)\lvert k_i\rangle.\end{aligned}$ The annihilation operator turns the initial one-particle ket into the vacuum, $a(k_i)\lvert k_i\rangle=\lvert0\rangle,$ and the creation operator, acting to the left, turns the final one-particle bra into the vacuum bra, $\langle k_f\rvert a^\dagger(k_f)=\langle0\rvert.$ Their matrix element is therefore $\langle0\vert0\rangle=1.$ All the operator bookkeeping reduces to one; the coupling supplies $g$, and the nontrivial part left over is the time integral: $\mathcal A_{i\to f}
=g\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}dt\,
e^{i(\omega_f-\omega_i)t}.$

